#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166601 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "37fde5f8-b1e8-5903-b875-94b4403cb282" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability at a Point"}] :question/id #uuid "b301b73e-1d26-56b7-bb21-33d6acae8d5d" :question/math-academy-id "e-8557" :question/problem "Given that $X \\sim B(6, 0.7),$ compute $P(X = 2).$ Round your final answer to $4$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "For a binomial random variable $X \\sim B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial,  we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x) =\n\\displaystyle \\binom{n}{x} p^x (1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X \\sim B(6, 0.7),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x) =\n\\displaystyle \\binom{6}{x} (0.7)^x (0.3)^{6-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=2) & =f(2) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{2})(0.7)^{2}(0.3)^{6-2} \\\\  & =15(0.7)^{2}(0.3)^{4} \\\\  & ≈0.0595\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto $4$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166602 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f98ff81-db37-590b-8910-58928af97f44" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability Over a Bounded Interval"}] :question/id #uuid "449e6e8f-0e9b-5208-8945-1ce3ea3d3dbd" :question/math-academy-id "e-8558" :question/problem "Given $X \\sim B(5, 0.3),$ compute $P(0 <     X < 3).$ Round your final answer to $4$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "For a binomial random variable $X \\sim B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial,  we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x) =\n\\displaystyle \\binom{n}{x} p^x (1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X \\sim B(5, 0.3),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x) =\n\\displaystyle \\binom{5}{x} (0.3)^x (0.7)^{5-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0<X<3) & =P(X\\in {1,2}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{1})(0.3)^{1}(0.7)^{5-1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{2})(0.3)^{2}(0.7)^{5-2} \\\\  & =5(0.3)(0.7)^{4}+10(0.3)^{2}(0.7)^{3} \\\\  & ≈0.6689\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nto $4$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166603 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4844e17-9365-5a6f-a5f4-b9fa83fd81e1" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability Over an Interval Using the Complement"}] :question/id #uuid "ae2b59a4-d3d6-53ea-aca2-93cbb2da7478" :question/math-academy-id "e-8559" :question/problem "Given that $X \\sim B(7, 0.2),$ compute $P(X \\geq 2).$ Round your final answer to $4$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "For a binomial random variable $X \\sim B(n,p),$ we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x) =\n\\displaystyle \\binom{n}{x} p^x (1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X \\sim B(7, 0.2),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x) =\n\\displaystyle \\binom{7}{x} (0.2)^x (0.8)^{7-x}.\n$$\n\nComputing $P(X \\geq 2)$ will require us to compute $f(x)$ at $x=2, 3, 4, \\ldots, 7.$ However, if we compute the complement $P(X < 2)$ instead, then we will only have to compute $f(x)$ at $x=0, 1,$ which will result in a much faster computation.\n\nComputing $P(X < 2),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<2) & =P(X\\in {0,1}) \\\\  & =f(0)+f(1) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{0})(0.2)^{0}(0.8)^{7-0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{1})(0.2)^{1}(0.8)^{7-1} \\\\  & =(0.8)^{7}+7(0.2)(0.8)^{6} \\\\  & ≈0.5767\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 2) & =1-P(X<2) \\\\  & ≈1-0.5767 \\\\  & =0.4233\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166604 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a03ad88-dc53-582c-b2ed-1aeb0ee2d0f5" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Binomial Probability Over an Unbounded Interval"}] :question/id #uuid "aeaecf43-9fce-552c-a3f8-69a2f09f2d9c" :question/math-academy-id "e-8564" :question/problem "Given that $X \\sim B(10, 0.7),$ compute $P(X > 8).$ Round your final answer to $4$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "For a binomial random variable $X \\sim B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial,  we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x) =\n\\displaystyle \\binom{n}{x} p^x (1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X \\sim B(10, 0.7),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x) =\n\\displaystyle \\binom{10}{x} (0.7)^x (0.3)^{10-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>8) & =P(X\\in {9,10}) \\\\  & =f(9)+f(10) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{9})(0.7)^{9}(0.3)^{10-9}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{10})(0.7)^{10}(0.3)^{10-10} \\\\  & =10(0.7)^{9}(0.3)+1(0.7)^{10}(0.3)^{0} \\\\  & ≈0.1493\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto $4$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186361155 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a03ad88-dc53-582c-b2ed-1aeb0ee2d0f5" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Binomial Probability Over an Unbounded Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "475522b5-b9ab-5f52-addb-3016e2b81d27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{243}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361157} {:answer/id #uuid "808edd68-8244-5265-83f8-e3dd74b60385" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361158} {:answer/id #uuid "e9733933-7a54-5039-adac-c9c416b22f37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{81}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361159} {:answer/id #uuid "e70384ce-2863-5997-9d9b-4c1521e8df3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361160} {:answer/id #uuid "c5fe4d64-e876-53a8-a0a4-c18010d17887" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{81}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361161}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "808edd68-8244-5265-83f8-e3dd74b60385" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361158} :answer-field/id #uuid "7399f40d-0bf8-58d4-a60f-61340aae26d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361156}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1a4ce7ee-5f92-57ab-9699-cb09f187adb6" :question/math-academy-id "q-129971" :question/problem "Given that $X∼B(4,\\frac{1}{3}),$ compute $P(X<3).$" :question/worked-solution "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial,  we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(4,\\frac{1}{3}),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{x}){(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{4-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<3) & =P(X\\in {0,1,2}) \\\\  & =f(0)+f(1)+f(2) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{0}){(\\frac{1}{3})}^{0}{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{4-0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{1}){(\\frac{1}{3})}^{1}{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{4-1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{2}){(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{4-2} \\\\  & =1{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{0}{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{4}+4{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{1}{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{3}+6{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186361162 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f98ff81-db37-590b-8910-58928af97f44" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability Over a Bounded Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43a18574-cf2c-5c3d-a905-6d1a75075347" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{65}{81}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361164} {:answer/id #uuid "57a2e276-4c45-51f0-9a7f-29286e3d247a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{160}{243}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361165} {:answer/id #uuid "6eaaed16-c6b9-5192-827f-b06daa333b8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{83}{243}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361166} {:answer/id #uuid "37144c2a-aa8e-5bb3-b38e-b2f1f8d2d6a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{81}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361167} {:answer/id #uuid "baeebe31-9bd7-54e2-bf89-75703e359dd2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{243}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361168}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57a2e276-4c45-51f0-9a7f-29286e3d247a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{160}{243}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361165} :answer-field/id #uuid "aeee24c4-81bf-542a-9a1c-2c0b5af3ea1f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361163}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "251bb292-8556-5b99-b0e3-0dda73534412" :question/math-academy-id "q-129973" :question/problem "Given $X∼B(5,\\frac{1}{3}),$ compute $P(1\\le X\\le 2).$" :question/worked-solution "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial,  we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(5,\\frac{1}{3}),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{x}){(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{5-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1\\le X\\le 2) & =P(X\\in {1,2}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{1}){(\\frac{1}{3})}^{1}{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{5-1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{2}){(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{5-2} \\\\  & =5{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{1}{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{4}+10{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{160}{243}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186361169 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "37fde5f8-b1e8-5903-b875-94b4403cb282" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4218977a-5760-5e34-85f7-1be6c434d037" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.247" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361171}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4218977a-5760-5e34-85f7-1be6c434d037" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.247" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361171} :answer-field/id #uuid "b50c249b-0237-5d75-ac45-d7a6951e7c72" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361170}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fbec0407-a14f-535d-94e9-5622f793f938" :question/math-academy-id "q-279626" :question/problem "If $X∼B(7,0.3),$ then, **rounded to $3$ decimal places**, $P(X=1)≈${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial, we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(7,0.3),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{x}){(0.3)}^{x}{(0.7)}^{7-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=1) & =f(1) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{1}){(0.3)}^{1}{(0.7)}^{7-1} \\\\  & =7{(0.3)}^{1}{(0.7)}^{6} \\\\  & ≈0.247\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186361172 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a03ad88-dc53-582c-b2ed-1aeb0ee2d0f5" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Binomial Probability Over an Unbounded Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d054f0df-7150-5ca4-ba38-3a0fca355244" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.157" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361174} {:answer/id #uuid "a4c373b2-6846-57fe-91e4-661b3e90f018" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.156" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361175}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a4c373b2-6846-57fe-91e4-661b3e90f018" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.156" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361175} :answer-field/id #uuid "db030ad3-a850-5509-b07d-29e82f7de5f0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361173}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c318c44f-85fb-5bf9-8eb6-be0c12f47d85" :question/math-academy-id "q-279689" :question/problem "If $X∼B(3,\\frac{3}{4}),$ then, **rounded to $3$ decimal places**, $P(X<2)≈${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial, we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(3,\\frac{3}{4}),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{x}){(\\frac{3}{4})}^{x}{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{3-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<2) & =P(X\\in {0,1}) \\\\  & =f(0)+f(1) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{0}){(\\frac{3}{4})}^{0}{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{3-0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{1}){(\\frac{3}{4})}^{1}{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{3-1} \\\\  & =1{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{0}{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{3}+3{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{1}{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2} \\\\  & ≈0.156\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186361176 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a03ad88-dc53-582c-b2ed-1aeb0ee2d0f5" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Binomial Probability Over an Unbounded Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c137a638-b505-528b-b6b9-9497934aa14b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.211" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361178} {:answer/id #uuid "c9ce2808-63c0-52d2-91ac-e5e4c5f64329" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.210" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361179}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c9ce2808-63c0-52d2-91ac-e5e4c5f64329" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.210" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361179} :answer-field/id #uuid "bbee166d-2db6-5b2f-ae47-3dcc4d488d44" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361177}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d98302b0-e333-52fa-a5fb-5d15d4a044ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-279694" :question/problem "If $X∼B(5,\\frac{2}{3}),$then, **rounded to $3$ decimal places**, $P(X<3)≈${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial, we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(5,\\frac{2}{3}),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{x}){(\\frac{2}{3})}^{x}{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{5-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<3) & =P(X\\in {0,1,2}) \\\\  & =f(0)+f(1)+f(2) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{0}){(\\frac{2}{3})}^{0}{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{5-0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{1}){(\\frac{2}{3})}^{1}{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{5-1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{2}){(\\frac{2}{3})}^{2}{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{5-2} \\\\  & =1{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{0}{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{5}+5{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{1}{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{4}+10{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{2}{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{3} \\\\  & ≈0.210\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186361180 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f98ff81-db37-590b-8910-58928af97f44" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability Over a Bounded Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54159eb5-1daa-5887-873f-c149fa6898fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.401" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361182}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54159eb5-1daa-5887-873f-c149fa6898fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.401" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361182} :answer-field/id #uuid "a4051e47-e068-5c2e-b08b-3d1baaef6a88" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361181}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "46475705-95e8-5e1f-99b3-822a4df8c2f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-279767" :question/problem "If $X∼B(5,0.1),$ then, **rounded to $3$ decimal places** $P(0<X\\le 2)≈${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial, we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(5,0.1),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{x})(0.1)^{x}(0.9)^{5-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0<X\\le 2) & =P(X\\in {1,2}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{1})(0.1)^{1}(0.9)^{5-1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{2})(0.1)^{2}(0.9)^{5-2} \\\\  & =5(0.1)^{1}(0.9)^{4}+10(0.1)^{2}(0.9)^{3} \\\\  & ≈0.401\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186361183 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4844e17-9365-5a6f-a5f4-b9fa83fd81e1" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability Over an Interval Using the Complement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f9d7644-b5be-5a7b-960b-ad7de7aba74a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.773" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361185}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3f9d7644-b5be-5a7b-960b-ad7de7aba74a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.773" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361185} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ae5b5b4-4842-5b3e-8b4e-83d1e9f5e302" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361184}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "368019ec-d338-512e-a617-3fb6fd7e61f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-279858" :question/problem "If $X∼B(7,\\frac{1}{2}),$  then, **rounded to $3$ decimal places**, $P(X\\ge 3)≈${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial, we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(7,\\frac{1}{2}),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{x}){(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{7-x}.\n$$\n\nComputing $P(X\\ge 3)$ will require us to compute $f(x)$ at $x=3,4,5,6,7.$ However, if we compute the complement $P(X<3)$ instead, then we will only have to compute $f(x)$ at $x=0,1,2,$ which will result in a much faster computation.\n\nComputing $P(X<3),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<3) & =P(X\\in {0,1,2}) \\\\  & =f(0)+f(1)+f(2) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{0}){(\\frac{1}{2})}^{0}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{7-0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{1}){(\\frac{1}{2})}^{1}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{7-1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{2}){(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{7-2} \\\\  & =1{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{0}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{7}+7{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{1}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{6}+21{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{5} \\\\  & ≈0.227\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 3) & =1-P(X<3) \\\\  & ≈1-0.227 \\\\  & =0.773\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186361186 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4844e17-9365-5a6f-a5f4-b9fa83fd81e1" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability Over an Interval Using the Complement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c38d3dd-b48e-549a-b5f1-fe1e16bf0ba7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.114" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361188} {:answer/id #uuid "c484769e-cb73-562f-94f7-eab54668f8f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.115" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361189}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5c38d3dd-b48e-549a-b5f1-fe1e16bf0ba7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.114" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361188} :answer-field/id #uuid "a71ef71f-7403-581a-834e-d2afffa7f218" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361187}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0b9ecd4f-c145-54ba-94bf-6399b3176fae" :question/math-academy-id "q-279981" :question/problem "If $X∼B(6,0.9),$ then, **rounded to $3$ decimal places**, $P(X<5)≈${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial, we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(6,0.9),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{x})(0.9)^{x}(0.1)^{6-x}.\n$$\n\nComputing $P(X<5)$ will require us to compute $f(x)$ at $x=0,1,2,3,4.$ However, if we compute the complement $P(X\\ge 5)$ instead, then we will only have to compute $f(x)$ at $x=5,6,$ which will result in a much faster computation.\n\nComputing $P(X\\ge 5),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 5) & =P(X\\in {5,6}) \\\\  & =f(5)+f(6) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{5})(0.9)^{5}(0.1)^{6-5}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{6})(0.9)^{6}(0.1)^{6-6} \\\\  & =6(0.9)^{5}(0.1)+(0.9)^{6} \\\\  & ≈0.886\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<5) & =1-P(X\\ge 5) \\\\  & ≈1-0.886 \\\\  & ≈0.114\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186361190 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4844e17-9365-5a6f-a5f4-b9fa83fd81e1" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability Over an Interval Using the Complement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c768b3d-749b-593a-97f8-943c0daf9bc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.423" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361192}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4c768b3d-749b-593a-97f8-943c0daf9bc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.423" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361192} :answer-field/id #uuid "0931eb65-b00c-55f4-ac00-9ddb13d8c3e1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361191}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1b7f280d-8d1f-5737-92f5-31c5c2c5d7d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-279984" :question/problem "If $X∼B(7,0.8),$ then, **rounded to $3$ decimal places**, $P(X<6)≈${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial, we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(7,0.8),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{x})(0.8)^{x}(0.2)^{7-x}.\n$$\n\nComputing $P(X<6)$ will require us to compute $f(x)$ at $x=0,1,2,…,5.$ However, if we compute the complement $P(X\\ge 6)$ instead, then we will only have to compute $f(x)$ at $x=6,7,$ which will result in a much faster computation.\n\nComputing $P(X\\ge 6),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 6) & =P(X\\in {6,7}) \\\\  & =f(6)+f(7) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{6})(0.8)^{6}(0.2)^{7-6}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{7})(0.8)^{7}(0.2)^{7-7} \\\\  & =7(0.8)^{6}(0.2)+(0.8)^{7} \\\\  & ≈0.577\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<6) & =1-P(X\\ge 6) \\\\  & ≈1-0.577 \\\\  & =0.423\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186361193 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4844e17-9365-5a6f-a5f4-b9fa83fd81e1" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability Over an Interval Using the Complement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "428354be-3dff-54eb-b8ef-e602571fd516" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.992" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361195}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "428354be-3dff-54eb-b8ef-e602571fd516" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.992" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361195} :answer-field/id #uuid "e38cf090-c129-585d-904f-06f9124c5eb3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361194}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c028ef54-6121-57ea-ad64-cc5a3f2b20f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-281208" :question/problem "If $X∼B(12,\\frac{2}{3}),$ then, **rounded to $3$ decimal places**, $P(X\\le 11)≈${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(12,\\frac{2}{3}),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{12}{x}){(\\frac{2}{3})}^{x}{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{12-x}.\n$$\n\nComputing $P(X\\le 11)$ will require us to compute $f(x)$ at $x=0,1,2,…,11.$ However, if we compute the complement $P(X>11)$ instead, then we will only have to compute $f(x)$ at $x=12,$ which will result in a much faster computation.\n\nComputing $P(X>11),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>11) & =P(X\\in {12}) \\\\  & =f(12) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{12}{12}){(\\frac{2}{3})}^{12}{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{12-12} \\\\  & =1{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{12}{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{0} \\\\  & ≈0.008\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 11) & =1-P(X>11) \\\\  & ≈1-0.008 \\\\  & =0.992\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167417 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "37fde5f8-b1e8-5903-b875-94b4403cb282" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80b2e102-cb56-5e08-b77f-7abec5470b8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0256" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166051} {:answer/id #uuid "010db3d6-a014-5d17-8548-d15e9a8ba1e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1274" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166052} {:answer/id #uuid "0153ac21-e4fe-570d-850e-96f9991d89cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0825" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166053} {:answer/id #uuid "2cae1324-5459-55e2-97b7-d72626cc2e93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1028" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166054} {:answer/id #uuid "67236a07-54e5-53a8-8563-cff732445f3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0521" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166055}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80b2e102-cb56-5e08-b77f-7abec5470b8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0256" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166051} :answer-field/id #uuid "95e8ccc7-9b28-55d0-952d-f4ef191f3d56" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166050}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "72224308-b2b4-58b8-968e-32f587f6cb16" :question/math-academy-id "q-96728" :question/problem "Given that $X∼B(4,0.2),$ compute $P(X=3).$ Round your final answer to $4$ decimal places." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial,  we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(4,0.2),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{x})(0.2)^{x}(0.8)^{4-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=3) & =f(3) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{3})(0.2)^{3}(0.8)^{4-3} \\\\  & =4(0.2)^{3}(0.8) \\\\  & ≈0.0256\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto $4$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186361196 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "37fde5f8-b1e8-5903-b875-94b4403cb282" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b155974d-c34f-57f6-946c-44c551fe2249" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1792" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361198} {:answer/id #uuid "1ad8c383-ac77-5c25-a721-7191117bd471" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2765" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361199} {:answer/id #uuid "aff190c3-45ef-5b3f-97df-98069e7f96e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2443" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361200} {:answer/id #uuid "5b75be13-1422-54e1-b280-4dd3462b1abb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3208" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361201} {:answer/id #uuid "6ecc152b-c735-57ff-9a71-8628dd46a509" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3557" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361202}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1ad8c383-ac77-5c25-a721-7191117bd471" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2765" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361199} :answer-field/id #uuid "6668f21e-b78f-5baf-b143-cb44e2141396" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361197}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e6f22b06-067a-5b42-b042-ed13e9f4696d" :question/math-academy-id "q-97464" :question/problem "Given that $X∼B(6,0.6),$ compute $P(X=3).$ Round your final answer to $4$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial,  we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(6,0.6),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{x})(0.6)^{x}(0.4)^{6-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=3) & =f(3) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{3})(0.6)^{3}(0.4)^{6-3} \\\\  & =20(0.6)^{3}(0.4)^{3} \\\\  & ≈0.2765\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto $4$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186361203 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "37fde5f8-b1e8-5903-b875-94b4403cb282" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d0f5ac78-1f20-5910-b2b9-e22670875540" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3446" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361205} {:answer/id #uuid "fa208ad7-2365-511e-820d-79bae951b418" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3011" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361206} {:answer/id #uuid "e7d9d871-5e83-5d5c-8995-